---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Agent Plugins compatibility"
  description="The Agent Plugins v1.0.0 compatibility target, current implementation status, and safety requirements for Veasel Code."
>
  <section class="doc-page">
    <p class="kicker">EXTENSIBILITY / COMPATIBILITY</p>
    <h1>
      One portable format.
      <br />
      <em>A careful host.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel Code targets the released Agent Plugins v1.0.0 specification. A
      compatible host must understand packages, Skills, and MCP configuration,
      then add its own clear trust and permission controls.
    </p>
    <div class="compatibility-banner">
      <span class="compatibility-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        ◌
      </span>
      <div>
        <strong>Local Agent Plugins READY · no remote marketplace claim</strong>
        <p>
          Veasel loads local packages (including durable{" "}
          <code>/plugin add [--replace]</code> directory links with install
          summaries), activates validated Skills per session, toggles package
          components, and exposes trusted stdio or Streamable HTTP MCP servers
          as model tools behind per-call review. Active Skills can expose
          bounded UTF-8 resources from their own directory for on-demand
          reading. Experimental <code>allowed-tools</code> declarations restrict
          the exact tool names available to the session; they cannot add tools
          or bypass approvals. Skill text and resource files are untrusted, and
          scripts are not run automatically. Veasel does not fetch remote plugin
          archives or claim an upstream marketplace.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What the format brings</h2>
      <div class="guide-grid">
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">{}</span>
          <h3>Plugin package</h3>
          <p>
            A portable directory starts with a versioned{" "}
            <code>plugin.json</code> manifest.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">✳</span>
          <h3>Agent Skills</h3>
          <p>
            Discover descriptions first, activate instructions per session, and
            read bounded text resources from that Skill when needed.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">⌘</span>
          <h3>MCP servers</h3>
          <p>
            Declare stdio or Streamable HTTP connections in a package’s{" "}
            <code>mcp.json</code>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Try session Skills and MCP servers</h2>
      <p>
        Place a package directory containing <code>plugin.json</code> under
        <code>$VEASEL_DATA_DIR/plugins</code>, or set
        <code>VEASEL_PLUGIN_DIR</code> to another directory. Start the local
        backend and open a session in the TUI.
      </p>
      <pre><code>/plugin add /path/to/my-plugin
/plugin add --replace /path/to/updated-plugin
/plugin remove my-plugin
/skills
/skill on review-tools/review
/skill off review-tools/review
/mcp
/mcp trust review-tools/local
/mcp untrust review-tools/local

# Or add a standalone configured server (no plugin package):
veasel mcp add stdio memory npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-memory
veasel mcp add project stdio repo-tools ./bin/mcp-server
/mcp trust configured/memory</code></pre>
      <p>
        Local package links use <code>/v1/plugins/links</code> and capability{" "}
        <code>plugins.links</code>; they never download remote archives.
        Standalone MCP config uses <code>/v1/mcp-servers</code> (user, project,
        and read-only <code>.mcp.json</code> import) — see the{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/docs/mcp/`}>MCP guide</a>. The plugin and Skill
        names come from the validated package manifest and directory. Manifest
        metadata fields such as description, homepage, repository, license,
        version, author, and keywords reject C0/DEL control bytes. Skill
        selection and MCP trust persist with the session. Disabling a Skill
        removes its instructions from the next model turn. MCP tools from
        trusted servers are available to the model, but every requested call
        pauses for review. Use <code>/mcp calls</code> or Ctrl+R to open the
        review panel, then select the call to inspect, approve, or reject it.
        Rejected calls are not retried. While a Skill is active, the model may
        read UTF-8 files only from that Skill’s directory; path traversal and
        symlink escapes are rejected. Script files are never executed
        automatically. A declared <code>allowed-tools</code> list restricts
        exact Veasel tool names already available to the session; it cannot
        grant MCP trust or bypass file and shell approvals. When multiple active
        Skills declare the field, their tool names combine. An empty list denies
        all tools; a missing declaration adds no restriction. Skill instructions
        and resources remain untrusted input.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Still open (not blocking local READY)</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          Remote marketplace install/update and an upstream package registry
          (local durable links + component toggles already ship).
        </li>
        <li>
          Automatic browser launch for MCP OAuth (PKCE authorize/complete, named
          credentials, and loopback <code>--listen</code> already ship — see the{" "}
          <a href={`${basePath}/docs/mcp/`}>MCP guide</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          Upstream-published cross-implementation fixtures (none at the pinned
          v1.0.0 revision); Veasel’s CI-gated local corpus remains the evidence
          base.
        </li>
        <li>
          Binary Skill assets and Skill-script execution with explicit approval
          are not supported. Trusted MCP stdio defaults to
          <code>VEASEL_MCP_STDIO_SANDBOX=require</code>: Linux Bubblewrap, macOS
          Seatbelt, or Windows AppContainer is required, and a missing backend
          refuses to start. Explicit <code>auto</code> permits unsandboxed
          fallback and <code>off</code> disables wrapping (honest
          <code>mcp.stdio.sandbox.*</code> capabilities; approved shell uses
          separate knobs).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Why compatibility is more than parsing.</strong> A plugin may
      start a local process or send data to a remote endpoint. Veasel must make
      those effects visible and keep them inside user-approved boundaries.
    </aside>
    <p class="doc-next">
      <a class="text-link" href="https://agent-plugins.org/specification">
        Read the Agent Plugins specification <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/compatibility/agent-plugins.md"
      >
        Review the conformance matrix <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/mcp/`}>
        MCP user guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/security/`}>
        See the security boundary <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
